────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Dependency on LibrePower core
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

This integration requires and imports directly from the ``librepower`` core
integration (also GPLv3-or-later, same authorship). ``powerwall.py`` imports
``BatteryClient``, ``BatterySnapshot``, ``BatteryCapabilities``, and the
generic battery exception types from ``custom_components.librepower.battery``
at runtime, via the shared ``custom_components`` namespace package. This is
GPL-to-GPL, so there is no license-compatibility question here (unlike the
MIT exception documented in core's own NOTICE) - both repositories carry the
same license.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Runtime dependency (not vendored, not modified)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

This project depends on, but does not include the source of:
  - pypowerwall (MIT, Copyright Jason Cox) - local Powerwall TEDAPI/v1r
    transport. Full credit for the actual protocol work belongs there; this
    repository only wraps it as a thin adapter conforming to core's contract.

Depending on a permissively-licensed library at runtime imposes no GPL
obligation on that library itself; GPL's copyleft applies to this project's
own source, not to libraries invoked through an ordinary dependency
mechanism.
